    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d95">

                <pb facs="0097" n="85" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg85"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/23091: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Egypt</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-07-25T17:00:00+03:00">July 25, 1942—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 7:44 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1317. Personal for the Secretary and Under Secretary. The continuation of the
                    hitherto indecisive fighting on the El Alamein front has furnished occasion for
                    professing of optimism as to the outcome of the struggle and an inclination is
                    apparent to exalt Allied achievement and discount the growing strength of the
                    enemy. This attitude is unjustified and fundamentally unhealthy. Reports are
                    current of <pb facs="0098" n="86" xml:id="pg86"/> the mounting reinforcements to
                    Rommel’s army and in spite of the efforts which have apparently been made, there
                    is no outstanding improvement in the general position of the British. A few days
                    ago operations were initiated by Auchinleck<anchor n="27" xml:id="d95-f1"/>
                    which it is difficult to believe were not intended to attain a decisive
                    objective but those operations halted at an inconclusive state. It is now said
                    that they may be resumed at any moment. I fail to see, however, any new and
                    definite factor which in itself offers encouragement for the future.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d95-f1" n="27" xml:id="d95-f1-content">
                <p>Gen. Sir Claude J. E. Auchinleck, Commanding General, British Forces in the
                    Middle East.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
